#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189104} :activity/id #uuid "cd68d011-8836-5efd-ab3b-ba09c700a3e9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049739 :topic/id #uuid "0fe4fee1-477c-5881-86c7-1921fd5fff4e" :topic/math-academy-id 3860} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189054 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188257 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188203 :question/id #uuid "012abf23-a2df-5479-a38f-3c76c099a39f" :question/math-academy-id "e-524"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d868c89-ca9e-589d-917e-cfec472a44f6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050411}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188542} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188543} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373820} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373834} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373841} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501193}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Two-Dimensional Related Rates Using Differential Equations"} :step/id #uuid "da66f4c9-69eb-512a-b43d-9289b5f8fb5a" :step/math-academy-id 1853 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189107}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189104 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189375 :tutorial/content "When constructing a differential equation to model a particular phenomenon, we sometimes combine several pieces of information to create the correct model.\n\nConsider the following situation:\n\n*The common cold virus spreads through a particular country. The rate at which the number of infected people increases is **proportional** to the **square of the time** $t$ (in days) since the outbreak started. \nAdditionally, $100$ infected people **recover from the virus each day**.*\n\nWe want to find a differential equation that models $P(t),$ the number of infected people. We note the following:\n\n- The statement describes the *rate* at which the *number of infected people* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d P}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\n- The rate at which the number of infected people increases is **proportional** to the **square of the time** $t.$ Therefore, if $I(t)$ represents the rate at which infections increase, then\n\n$$\nI(t) = kt^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a positive constant.\n\n- Additionally, $100$ infected people **recover from the virus each day**. Therefore, if $D(t)$ represents the rate at which infections decrease, then\n\n$$\nD(t) = 100.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the differential equation that describes this phenomenon is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t} & ={{k{t}^{2}-100}_{⏟}}_{I(t)-D(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlthough we introduced the functions $I(t)$ and $D(t)$ here, we can usually write down the appropriate differential equation straight away. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "8a7ad3b5-e4de-56c0-8fc9-8a9b2c976094" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6270 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "37206e3d-9800-567e-82f6-eed74a977f1f" :step/math-academy-id 38001 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189105}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189105 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188290 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186187994 :question/id #uuid "67d01a20-4e4b-5b03-a2a3-f4b21cd81662" :question/math-academy-id "e-10072"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9609247-0e03-57ea-b5ae-e4d8317f21cd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048746} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048835}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188913} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373715} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375089} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501160}] :knowledge-point/title "Translating Contextual Problems Into Differential Equations"} :step/id #uuid "9da430dc-8eca-5286-8278-c764eba32b5d" :step/math-academy-id 38002 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189245}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189107 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188291 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186187995 :question/id #uuid "1b0483b6-a42d-51fb-9ccd-fe6cb2a7acc1" :question/math-academy-id "e-10075"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba211dba-23ad-5755-b1e3-082e13be59a2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048542}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188912} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373708} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373806} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501204}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Three-Dimensional Related Rates Using Differential Equations"} :step/id #uuid "53525e67-0e77-588f-88a9-1bda7f5964de" :step/math-academy-id 38012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189108 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188292 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186187996 :question/id #uuid "b657adbb-c3c4-54c2-8c2c-44ccc0bf9c3a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10076"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "474f9c82-4ae7-58e0-9457-6bda9cab8973" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049688} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050975}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188916} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373792} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373862} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373876} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501219}] :knowledge-point/title "Translating Coordinate Plane Problems Into Differential Equations"} :step/id #uuid "ccce909c-08ed-53ce-a30b-e357e8de3d59" :step/math-academy-id 38016 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189054}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189245 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189439 :tutorial/content "Let's recall some basic facts from geometry in the $xy$-plane:\n\n- The distance between the point $(x,y)$ and the origin $(0,0)$ equals $\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}.$\n\n- The (smallest) distance between the point $(x,y)$ and the $x$-axis equals $|y|.$\n\n- Similarly, the (smallest) distance between the point $(x,y)$ and the $y$-axis equals $|x|.$\n\n- The slope of the line that passes through $(x,y)$ and the origin equals $\\dfrac{y}{x}.$\n\nWe sometimes need to use these basic facts when constructing differential equations described in a geometric context. \n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "b03d6daa-af90-5f14-bf0f-929da8526673" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8152 :tutorial/title "Recalling Some Facts From Elementary Geometry"} :step/id #uuid "d499c1d3-7173-5509-b128-c42561cce342" :step/math-academy-id 53348 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189108}}] :activity/title "Further Modeling With First-Order ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186188455}]}
